        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>This investigation is aimed at determining the effects of space radiation combined with microgravity on the function of human blood vessels and capillaries. The average human body contains tens of thousands of miles of vessels that permeate every tissue down to the microscopic level. The vascular system is crucial to healthy functioning of the tissues. Its dysfunction is not only a primary event in a range of degenerative diseases, but also an important influencing factor in many others. Two functions of the human vascular system that greatly affect human health and disease are:  1. the growth of new vessels to replace damaged vessels (angiogenesis), and 2. the process that allows nutritious molecules to cross from the blood to tissues and waste molecules to be cleared out from tissues (endothelial barrier function). Disruption of these processes is known to cause degenerative disease.
<div>
Previous studies have shown that the human vasculature is an important target for radiation and it is also influenced by gravitational forces. Specifically, by using human endothelial cells in 2 and 3-dimensional human tissue models, it has been shown that space radiation inhibits angiogenesis and disrupts endothelial barrier function. Because the doses and time course for radiation-induced events are now known, it possible to assay for joint effects with other environmental influences. Since angiogenesis and barrier function are also affected by microgravity, there is a potential for further dysfunction of the human vasculature when applied in combination with radiation.
</div><div>
The objectives of this study are to use simulated microgravity to:
</div><ol>
<li>Determine the combined effects of space radiation and microgravity on human blood vessel models. </li>
<li>Determine the impact of the combination of space radiation and microgravity on degeneration by testing for angiogenesis and endothelial barrier function using previously established assays. </li>
</ol>
<div>
APPROACH:<br> The approach for this investigation is unavailable.
</div><div>
RESULTS:<br>Results from this investigation were not completed. Please contact LSDA for further assistance. 
</div><div></div>
